Interesting — the route returned no-op for all edges because they were already orthogonal AND... wait — route should still add avoidance waypoints where the rendered path crosses nodes. The model says "no-op 说明几何已是最优" — but my route op: for edges already orthogonal (style check passes → skip style edit) AND `already_routed` false → it should still compute waypoints IF the direct path is blocked... 

Hmm wait — why no-op? Let me check: waypoints are only added when bends found AND geo_line is self-closing. For the edge passing through Agent node (e_file_canvas?), the DIRECT center-line might be clear (obstacle Agent node NOT on the center line!) — the occlusion comes from the RENDERER's L-bend, not the straight line. My waypoint candidates: direct clear → no bends. So route correctly did nothing — the occlusion through Agent node comes from orthogonal rendering of a center-clear line: renderer connects terminals with L-shape that happens to pass through Agent.

Wait — actually let me reconsider: my candidates include L-shapes (which mimic renderer behavior) but I pick the FIRST CLEAR candidate = direct. If direct is "clear" per center-to-center line, I add no waypoints — but the renderer with orthogonal style draws an L (not the direct line!) and that L may hit the node. My model of renderer behavior is wrong: with orthogonalEdgeStyle and no waypoints/anchors, mxGraph OrthConnector routes via mid-bends, NOT straight. So "direct clear" ≠ rendered path clear.

Fix: when style is (or becomes) orthogonal, the path model should be the L-shapes/Z the renderer would draw — i.e., prefer adding waypoints whenever ANY candidate has bends and the direct L-render is blocked... More precisely: for orthogonal edges, we should evaluate the RENDERER's likely path (some L-shape we don't control) — since we can't predict it exactly, the robust move: ALWAYS set explicit waypoints for orthogonal edges using our candidate search (so the path is fully determined by our points), choosing the best clear candidate including direct-ish paths (e.g. L with single bend, or Z). Only if ALL candidates blocked → leave to renderer.

Change: for edges that are orthogonal (or being made orthogonal), don't special-case "direct" as no-op — use candidate list where the first entry "direct" (no bends) corresponds to straight line which renderer won't draw for orthogonal style anyway... Hmm — wait: with orthogonalEdgeStyle, a direct center-line that needs no bend gets drawn as... OrthConnector still makes right angles: if endpoints are aligned vertically/horizontally it's straight; otherwise it bends. So for orthogonal edges, "no waypoints" means the renderer picks its own bends — unpredictable.

Revised logic: if edge is (or becomes) orthogonal:
- compute candidates as before but EXCLUDING the empty candidate (since renderer won't honor a straight path anyway)... but if endpoints are axis-aligned (same x or same y), straight IS what renderer draws. Condition: if sc.x==tc.x or sc.y==tc.y → straight is safe → no waypoints. Otherwise, find first non-empty clear candidate; if found → set waypoints (renderer honors them); if none → leave to renderer.
- if edge is NOT orthogonal (plain): direct straight line will be honored → current behavior fine (empty candidate first).

So update the route branch: choose candidate list based on whether the edge is/gets orthogonal:
```rust
let mut cands = if orthogonal || becomes_orthogonal {
    vec![L-v, L-h, Z-my, Z-mx, above, below, right, left]  // no empty
    // unless axis-aligned: vec![] first
} else {
    vec![empty, L-v, L-h, ...]
};
```
If axis-aligned (sc.x==tc.x || sc.y==tc.y) → prepend empty.

Also the model said "route 返回 no-op 说明几何已是最优" — with my change, route on the Agent-crossing edge would set waypoints and actually fix the occlusion.

But wait — the run also showed the model once again being too credulous: it accepted "no-op = 最优" without cross-checking against its own view that showed the file→canvas edge crossing 服务层 region. The view one-liner likely said crossings warnings exist but model wrapped up declaring acceptable. Fine.

Also the user's complaint "线太乱遮挡太多" — with the waypoint fix we can actually resolve the Agent-node occlusion. Let me implement:

In route branch, currently:
```rust
let already_routed = edge_geom.is_some_and(|e| !e.points.is_empty());
if !already_routed {
    if let (Some(e), Some(src), Some(dst)) = (...) {
        let bends = route_waypoints(src, dst, &obstacles_for(...));
        if !bends.is_empty() && geo_line.trim_end().ends_with("/>") { ... }
    }
}
```

Change to: pass a flag `require_bends` (edge is or becomes orthogonal and not axis-aligned) into a modified route_waypoints that skips the empty candidate:

```rust
fn route_waypoints(src, dst, obstacles, allow_direct: bool) -> Vec<(f64,f64)> {
    ...
    let mut candidates = vec![ L-v, L-h, Z, Z, above, below, right, left ];
    if allow_direct { candidates.insert(0, vec![]); }
    // axis-aligned: straight is what renderer draws
    if sc.0 == tc.0 || sc.1 == tc.1 { candidates.insert(0, vec![]); }
    candidates.into_iter().find(|b| path_ok(b)).unwrap_or_default()
}
```

Where allow_direct = NOT orthogonal-after-this-call. In the branch: `let will_be_orthogonal = cell_line.contains("edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle") || just_set_flag;` Track whether we added the style edit for this edge.

Note: bends chosen must keep collinear-redundancy removed (L paths have 1 bend — the 2-bend Z with equal coordinates could produce duplicate bends... e.g. Z-my with my==sc.y? only when axis-aligned; then empty candidate wins anyway).

Also — dedupe consecutive identical bends (could happen: Z-mx with mx == sc.x when aligned — covered).

Update tests: layout_route_clear_path_skips_waypoints — the edge is NOT orthogonal initially and route makes it orthogonal! With new logic: edge plain → becomes orthogonal → allow_direct=false (unless axis-aligned). Test fixture: a(0,0,100,50) b(400,0,100,50): sc=(50,25), tc=(450,25) — same y! axis-aligned → empty candidate first → no waypoints ✓ test still passes (no Array). Good.

